let __v_155 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"deeds\",{\"$tuple\":[\"The deeds are the title register, and HM Land Registry holds it for England and Wales (Registers of Scotland north of the border). Their own service will sell you a copy from the property's address, and that copy is the one that counts.\",\"What people call the deeds is now the title register at HM Land Registry, so there is nothing to ask a solicitor for first: search their service by the property's address, pay a few pounds, and the copy comes as a download.\"]}],[\"logbook\",{\"$tuple\":[\"The log book is the V5C and the DVLA issues it - no shop has one. If yours is lost or damaged, apply for a replacement on gov.uk with the car's details and the green slip you kept from the sale.\",\"That one comes from the DVLA, not a counter in town: a replacement V5C is applied for on gov.uk, and the car's registration number plus the details of the keeper is what the form asks for.\"]}],[\"will\",{\"$tuple\":[\"The will itself is with whoever drew it up or kept it - usually the solicitor - and once probate is granted a copy goes on the public record at the Probate Service. Ask the solicitor first, then search the Probate Service's own record.\",\"Start with the solicitor named on it, or the person who kept the original, because a will is not registered anywhere while the person is alive. After probate it is a public document and the Probate Service will sell you a copy.\"]}],[\"lease\",{\"$tuple\":[\"The lease is held by the landlord or their managing agent, and if you bought the flat your solicitor will have kept a copy - that is usually a phone call and an email. A managing agent will send one for a small fee if the solicitor cannot find it.\",\"Ask the landlord or the managing agent for a copy, and check your own paperwork first: anyone who has bought a leasehold flat was sent one at completion, and the solicitor who did the buying keeps it on file.\"]}],[\"certificate\",{\"$tuple\":[\"A certificate comes from the register office for the district where the birth, marriage or death was registered, or from the General Register Office for older ones. Their own site takes the order - have the date and the place ready.\",\"The register office for the district where it happened holds it, and the General Register Office holds the older records. Order through their own site with the date and the place, and the certificate comes by post.\"]}],[\"probate\",{\"$tuple\":[\"A grant of probate comes from the Probate Service, through gov.uk, and it is the court's copy rather than the will. An estate with a house, shares or a disagreement in it is worth a solicitor doing that part.\",\"Probate is the Probate Service's, applied for on gov.uk, and it is worth checking whether the estate is small enough for the simpler form. If there is property or a will being argued over, hand it to a solicitor.\"]}],[\"records\",{\"$tuple\":[\"Your medical records sit with the GP surgery you are registered at, and you can ask for a copy in writing - the surgery has to answer, usually within a month.\",\"Ask the GP surgery that has you registered: a written request gets you a copy of the records, and the reception will say what they need from you to prove it is you.\"]}]]}")?;
